{"y":1984,"label":"1983–84","coach":"Dom Perno","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":13,"l":15,"cw":5,"cl":11,"confFinish":"7th in Big East","finish":"none","srs":2.03,"sos":2.6,"ortg":95.1,"drtg":95.9,"ppg":71.1,"oppg":71.7,"story":{"headline":"Hobbs Leads as Injuries Sideline Earl Kelley","text":"Earl Kelley scored 37 points against Boston University in December, but ankle injuries limited the sophomore to 18 games, and the 1983–84 Huskies leaned on 5-foot-8 senior captain Karl Hobbs, who averaged 6.4 assists, made third-team All-Big East and left with a then-school-record 534 career assists. Juniors Alvin Frederick and Ray Broxton, sophomores Eddie Williams and Tim Coles and senior Vern Giscombe rounded out the rotation. UConn went 7–2 before Big East play, beating Columbia and Arizona State to win the Connecticut Mutual Classic, and won three of its first six league games, including a 79–58 rout at Pittsburgh. From there the Huskies won only three of their last 13. The most memorable night was a loss: an 87–85 triple-overtime defeat at No. 16 Syracuse before 29,254 fans in the Carrier Dome on February 18, 1984. Twice UConn sank free throws with no time left to force another overtime, and Giscombe, starting in place of the injured Kelley, played all 55 minutes and scored a career-high 24. UConn finished 13–15 and 5–11, losing its last four games, the final one 73–58 to Syracuse in the Big East quarterfinals at Madison Square Garden.","moments":[{"date":"1983-12-29","title":"Connecticut Mutual Classic champions","text":"UConn beats Arizona State 66–60 to win its holiday tournament at the Hartford Civic Center."},{"date":"1984-02-18","title":"Triple overtime at the Dome","text":"No. 16 Syracuse outlasts UConn 87–85 in three overtimes at the Carrier Dome; Vern Giscombe plays all 55 minutes and scores a career-high 24."}],"honors":["Karl Hobbs – Third-team All-Big East"],"sources":["https://uconnhuskies.com/documents/download/2026/8/13/RECORD_BOOK_26-27_V2_.p